\textbf{The fix.} Every decision a rule makes (similarities, distances, norms,
clip bounds, noise scales, selection scores) is computed on the trainable
coordinates $u_i$ only, while aggregation still averages the full vector so that
BatchNorm statistics remain synchronised. The fix is a mask, it changes no
rule's definition, and it lets the same code run in either mode. That is what
makes the controlled comparison of Sec.~\ref{sec:p1} possible.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

The experiments ask one question: holding everything else fixed, what does the
counter change in what each rule decides and in the model it returns?

\textbf{Task and data.} We use CICIoT2023~\cite{neto2023ciciot} in its grouped
8-class form ($39$ flow features), standardized with a scaler fit on the training
split, and stratified-subsampled to $40{,}000$ training and $10{,}000$ test rows
with at least $200$ rows per class.

\textbf{Model.} The detector treats the $39$-dimensional feature vector as a
single-channel sequence: a Conv1D layer ($64$ filters, kernel $3$, ReLU,
BatchNorm), an LSTM ($128$ hidden units) and a dense head ($64$ units, dropout
$0.3$) with softmax. It has one BatchNorm layer, so $L=1$ and the counter is a
single coordinate of the $\dfNTrain{}+\dfNBuf$ transmitted ones
(Table~\ref{tab:probe}).

\textbf{Federation.} $N=10$ clients, all participating in each of $R=15$
synchronous rounds; one local epoch per round with batch size $256$, Adam,
learning rate $10^{-3}$, and per-class loss weights from the effective number of
samples. Clients are partitioned IID, in equal shards of about $4{,}000$ rows (so every
client runs the same $16$ local batches per round), or by a Dirichlet($\alpha=0.5$)
label split, under which shard sizes, and hence the batch counts $B_i$, differ
between clients. Aggregation is performed by Flower~\cite{beutel2020flower}
\texttt{Strategy} objects driven by an in-process round loop, so each rule
receives the per-client \sd{} updates that Flower's PyTorch example would put on
the wire.

\textbf{Threat model.} \emph{Goal:} a targeted backdoor; trigger-carrying attack
traffic should be classified as benign while clean accuracy is preserved.
\emph{Capabilities:} in attacked runs a fraction $m=0.2$ of the clients (two of
ten) is malicious and colludes. Each malicious client stamps the \emph{fixed}
trigger of Table~\ref{tab:trig} on a fraction $p=0.1$ of its attack samples,
relabels them benign, and trains $E_m$ local epochs where honest clients train
one; it controls its own data and computation, not the server or honest clients,
and sends an update of the usual format. \emph{Knowledge:} architecture,
hyperparameters and trigger; it does not adapt to the rule's decisions.
\emph{Budget:} which clients are corrupted and $E_m$ are fixed by calibration under
FedAvg before any defended cell runs (below). The trigger perturbs standardized
features, a feature-space threat model whose distance from realizable traffic
Sec.~\ref{sec:realizable} measures.

\textbf{The counter is attacker-controlled.} Our attacker reports the batches it
ran, $c_i=B_i\mathbf{1}_L$, but nothing in a \sd{}-based pipeline enforces that,
and under the default momentum a falsified value costs the client nothing.
Under the defect, reporting the cohort's typical batch count removes the
$L(B_i-B_j)^2$ penalty of Corollary~\ref{cor:dist}, and against FLTrust a suitable
counter buys admission whatever the update's direction
(Corollaries~\ref{cor:fltrust} and~\ref{cor:falsify}). These are consequences of
the proposition checked on the implementation for one round; we have not trained
with a falsifying attacker. Under the fix the value is never read by a rule; it is
still averaged into the global counter, which is inert.

\textbf{Attack calibration.} At this scale the backdoor survives averaging with
eight honest updates only if the malicious clients train longer than honest ones.
Under FedAvg with one local epoch the attacked BSR stays at the clean floor
(non-IID: $\apDirPoneBSR$ against $\apDirCleanBSR$), and it stays there with five times
as many poisoned rows, a third attacker or a doubled trigger
(Table~\ref{tab:calib}). Under example-weighted aggregation two attackers drawn at
random hold $\csShareRandMin$ to $\csShareRandMax$ of the non-IID data depending on
the seed, which made attack strength a property of the partition draw. The re-run
therefore fixes the budget: two attackers whose combined share is within
$\csShareTol$ percentage point of $m$ (\texttt{attack.mal\_selection:
share\_matched}), training $E_m=15$ local epochs. That setting was chosen by a rule
fixed before its calibration finished (FedAvg implants, last-five-round BSR above
$0.5$, on at least eight of ten non-IID seeds); it implants on
$\csSmEfifteenNimpl$ of ten, seed $46$ being the exception
(Table~\ref{tab:seedcal}; Sec.~\ref{sup:calib} gives the calibration and why seed
$46$ fails). A rule's attacked BSR is read only on seeds where the re-run's own
FedAvg cell implants. The price is visibility: the attackers' trainable update
norm is $\csMalNormRatioMin$--$\csMalNormRatioMax$ times an honest client's.

\textbf{Server.} The server sees only the transmitted updates. FLTrust is the one
exception: it holds a class-balanced clean root set of $300$ training rows, which
at batch size $256$ is two mini-batches, so its own update has $B_0=2$.

\textbf{Metrics.} Clean accuracy (CA) and macro-F1 on the clean test set;
backdoor success rate (BSR), the fraction of triggered attack-class test samples
classified as benign; and, for rules that exclude clients, the detection rate (DR,
fraction of malicious clients excluded) and the false-positive rate (FPR, fraction
of honest clients excluded), always reported together. In clean runs every client
is honest, so FPR is the fraction of all clients a rule excludes, and BSR is the
clean-model rate at which the trigger alone moves attack traffic to benign: the
floor against which an attacked BSR must be read. Rules that never single out a
client report DR and FPR as not applicable. Every metric is the mean over the
last five rounds.

\textbf{Statistics.} Seeds $42$--$51$ fix the partition, the malicious clients,
the initialisation and the data order, and the two arms (Sec.~\ref{sec:p1}) share
them. For each rule, partition, attack setting and metric we report the arm means,
the paired mean difference fixed$-$defect, the number of seeds on which it is
positive and negative, and the exact two-sided $p$ of the paired sign-flip
permutation test, which enumerates all $2^n$ sign assignments and assumes only
symmetry of each seed's difference under no effect~\cite{ernst2004permutation};
the 95\% interval inverts the same test. The smallest attainable $p$ is $2^{1-n}$
($\pfTestableTen$ at ten seeds, above $0.05$ at five or fewer), so a cell is read
for significance only at $n\ge 6$.
\emph{Confirmatory and exploratory analyses are separate.} Before most cells ran we
registered (\path{iterations/PREREG_P3.md}, with a hash manifest of the
$\pfPreManifestN$ result files then on disk) the comparisons the theory signs: primary,
FLTrust's FPR (clean) and DR (attacked) in both partitions, lower in the defect arm
(Corollary~\ref{cor:fltrust}); secondary, the clean accuracy of NormClip and FLAME,
lower in the defect arm because~\eqref{eq:norm} inflates their noise scale. Each is
tested one-sided, Holm within its family ($\pfPrePrimaryTotal$ and $\pfPreSecondaryTotal$; floor $\pfPreFloorTen$ at ten
seeds). $\pfPrePartialN$ rest partly on seeds $42$--$46$, read before registration;
the other $\pfPreBlindN$ are blind and are also Holm-corrected alone. So far
($\pfPreFinal$) $\pfPrePrimarySupported$ primary and $\pfPreSecondarySupported$
secondary hypotheses are supported. All else is exploratory: two-sided, Holm across
the thirteen rules sharing a partition, attack setting and metric ($p_H$)~\cite{holm1979};
of its $\pfTestsRun$ tests, $\pfSigFamily$ survive that and
$\pfSigGlobal$ survive Holm over all of them (\texttt{paper/analyse\_postfix.py}).
Every outcome number in
Secs.~\ref{sec:setup}--\ref{sec:results} is a macro written by that script from the
result files; a cell that has not run prints as \PENDING. At the time of writing
$\pfCellsDone$ of the $\pfCellsTarget$ cells have completed.
% RE-CHECK every interpretive sentence in Sec. 6 when P3 completes (1,040 cells);
% the macros update themselves, the prose does not.

\textbf{Round-one aliasing.} The engine had a second defect, unrelated to the
counter: it stored the initial global model as views of the model's own parameter
storage, so in round~$1$, and only there, the clients trained one after another and
the rules received the last client's model as the round's reference. It is the
same in both arms and reaches outcomes only through the training trajectory, but
it does change FoolsGold, whose history carries the round-$1$ updates forward
(Sec.~\ref{sup:alias}). Every FoolsGold outcome of the first campaign is therefore
withdrawn. The re-run copies the stored model
(\texttt{federated.copy\_initial\_global}).


\section{Aggregation Rules and What the Decomposition Predicts}
\label{sec:rules}

We run thirteen rules. All subclass Flower's FedAvg strategy and differ only in
\texttt{aggregate\_fit}. Table~\ref{tab:rules} groups them by the statistic their
decisions read, which is what Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} is about, and states
the prediction for each before any outcome is seen.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{The thirteen rules, the statistic their client-level decisions read, and the
exposure to the counter that Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} predicts. ``Arms
identical'' means the rule reads no client-level statistic, so the defect and
fixed arms of Sec.~\ref{sec:p1} run the same computation and serve as negative
controls.}
\label{tab:rules}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.21\linewidth}p{0.44\linewidth}p{0.29\linewidth}@{}}
\toprule
rule & decision statistic & predicted exposure \\
\midrule
FedAvg~\cite{mcmahan2017} & none (example-weighted mean) & none; arms identical \\
Median, trimmed mean~\cite{yin2018} & per-coordinate order statistics & none; arms identical \\
Krum (Multi-Krum)~\cite{blanchard2017} & sum of squared distances to nearest neighbours & iff $B_i$ differ (Cor.~\ref{cor:dist}) \\
Bulyan~\cite{mhamdi2018bulyan} & iterated Krum selection, then trimmed mean & iff $B_i$ differ (Cor.~\ref{cor:dist}) \\
RFA~\cite{pillutla2022rfa} & Weiszfeld weights $1/\|d_i-z\|$ & iff $B_i$ differ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:dist}) \\
NormClip~\cite{sun2019} & clip to median norm, noise $\propto$ that norm & always (Eq.~\eqref{eq:norm}) \\
FLAME~\cite{nguyen2022flame} & cosine clustering; median-norm clip and noise & always (Cor.~\ref{cor:band}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:norm}) \\
FLTrust~\cite{cao2021fltrust} & cosine to server update; norm rescaling & always (Cor.~\ref{cor:fltrust}) \\
FoolsGold~\cite{fung2020foolsgold} & cosine between cumulative histories & always (Cor.~\ref{cor:band}) \\
GradCos & maximum pairwise cosine (clique test) & always (Cor.~\ref{cor:band}) \\
FLDetector~\cite{zhang2022fldetector} & error of a cross-round update prediction & not covered; measured only \\
AlignIns~\cite{xu2025alignins} & robust $z$ of cosine to $\theta_t$ (TDA) and of sign agreement (MPSA); median-norm clip & TDA and clip always; MPSA not \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\textbf{Implementation.} Sec.~\ref{sup:rules} describes each rule. FedAvg, the
median and the trimmed mean read no client-level statistic, so the two arms run
the same computation and their paired differences measure nondeterminism (negative
controls). Distance rules read the counter whenever batch counts differ: under
Dirichlet partitions, and in every attacked run, since an attacker training
$E_m=15$ epochs reports about $15$ times an honest client's count. For NormClip
and FLAME, Eq.~\eqref{eq:norm} sets clip bound and noise scale
$\pfDiagNormInflDir$ times larger (median buffer share, non-IID) than the trainable
update warrants. FLAME's noise is added to trainable coordinates only, in both arms
(noise on \texttt{running\_var} makes evaluation-mode BatchNorm return NaN).
FLTrust counts a client with weight zero as excluded, FoolsGold one with weight
below $10^{-3}$. GradCos is a clique test from an earlier version of this study,
kept only as one more cosine rule. AlignIns~\cite{xu2025alignins} (2025) is ported
from its authors' code; the decomposition predicts that its sign-agreement filter is
unaffected and its cosine filter and clip are exposed. Krum and Bulyan get $f=2$
(Bulyan's $f$ clamped, since $n\ge4f+3$ fails at $n=10$). Hyperparameters are the
untuned defaults of \texttt{configs/p1\_full.yaml}, identical in both arms, so
absolute performance should not be read from our tables.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{What the counter does to the transmitted geometry}
\label{sec:geometry}

Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry} shows, per round, what the rules are given in the
non-IID runs. The buffers carry almost all of the squared update norm and of the
pairwise squared distances (medians $\pfDiagBufNormShareDir$ and
$\pfDiagBufDistShareDir$ over $\pfDiagBufNormShareDirRuns$ runs), and the mean
pairwise cosine over the transmitted vector sits near $\pfDiagCosFullDir$ while
the trainable updates' is $\pfDiagCosSigDir$. All diagnostics are over rounds
$2$--$15$; round~$1$ describes the engine's round-one defect instead
(Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}; median full-vector cosine $\raPoneCosFullRone$ in round~$1$
against $\raPoneCosFullRtwo$ later, $\raPoneRuns$ clean non-IID runs).
Corollary~\ref{cor:dist} predicts that in IID runs, where every client runs the
same number of batches, the buffer share of the squared distances falls far below
its non-IID value while the share of the squared norm does not. The IID medians
are $\pfDiagBufDistShareIid$ and $\pfDiagBufNormShareIid$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_p1_geometry.pdf}
\caption{Per-round defect diagnostics in the re-run's runs, rounds $2$--$15$ (round~$1$
is omitted, see \emph{Round-one aliasing} in Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}; median over runs, shaded
inter-quartile range; logged in both arms and for every rule, because they
describe the transmitted vector rather than a rule's decision). Left: mean
pairwise client cosine over the full transmitted vector and over the trainable
coordinates. Right: buffer share of the squared update norm and of the pairwise
squared distances. Generated by \texttt{paper/make\_fig\_p1.py}.}
\label{fig:geometry}
\end{figure}

\subsection{What the counter changes: a controlled A/B}
\label{sec:p1}

\textbf{Design.} Each rule runs twice, in a \emph{defect} arm whose decisions read
the full transmitted vector and a \emph{fixed} arm whose decisions read only the
trainable coordinates (Sec.~\ref{sec:scope}), identical otherwise, seed included,
so the per-seed difference fixed$-$defect is the causal effect of the counter on
that rule. The campaign of record, the re-run, crosses thirteen rules, two
partitions, clean and attacked runs, and ten seeds ($1{,}040$ cells), with the
round-one copy and the seed-robust adversary (share-matched attackers, $E_m=15$).
Tables~\ref{tab:p1} (clean accuracy, BSR) and~\ref{tab:p1flag} (exclusions of the
eight rules that make them) are generated from its result files and fill as cells
complete ($\pfCellsDone$ of $\pfCellsTarget$ so far). The first campaign (random
attackers, $E_m=5$, round-one-defective engine, non-IID seeds $42$--$46$;
Sec.~\ref{sup:retro}) is quoted only where it bears on a mechanism, and labelled.

\textbf{Results so far.} \PENDING{} most cells of the re-run, including every
IID cell and every attacked cell of most defended rules.
% RETIRES WITH: P3 campaign (results/postfix_p3)
Four kinds of cell have landed for several seeds. At that size no paired exact
test can reach $p<0.05$ ($2^{1-n}=\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanPmin$ at $n=5$), so we
report direction and consistency across seeds, not significance.
(i) \emph{Negative control.} FedAvg's clean non-IID clean accuracy is
$\pfCAFedavgDirCleanDef$ and $\pfCAFedavgDirCleanFix$ in the two arms (paired
difference $\pfCAFedavgDirCleanDiff$, $n=\pfCAFedavgDirCleanN$): where the arms run
the same computation they reproduce each other.
% RE-CHECK "reproduce" against \pfCAFedavgDirCleanDiff as seeds land.
(ii) \emph{FoolsGold}, clean non-IID: clean accuracy $\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanDef$
(defect) and $\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanFix$ (fixed), difference
$\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanDiff$ (fixed higher on $\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanPos$ of
$\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanN$ seeds, exact $p=\pfCAFoolsgoldDirCleanP$); it excludes
$\pfFPRFoolsgoldDirCleanDef$ and $\pfFPRFoolsgoldDirCleanFix$ of an honest cohort,
the failure of its own assumption diagnosed in Sec.~\ref{sec:mech}; whether the
counter adds a cost on top is for the remaining seeds to decide.
% RE-CHECK when the FoolsGold Dir clean cells reach n=10.
(iii) \emph{FLAME}, clean non-IID ($n=\pfFPRFlameDirCleanN$): exclusions
$\pfFPRFlameDirCleanDef$ and $\pfFPRFlameDirCleanFix$ (difference
$\pfFPRFlameDirCleanDiff$; $\pfFPRFlameDirCleanPos$ seeds up,
$\pfFPRFlameDirCleanNeg$ down), clean accuracy difference $\pfCAFlameDirCleanDiff$
($\pfCAFlameDirCleanPos$ up, $\pfCAFlameDirCleanNeg$ down): indistinguishable arms
despite an exposed input.
% RE-CHECK the FLAME sentence when its Dir clean cells reach n=10 (P3).
(iv) \emph{FLTrust}, clean non-IID ($n=\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanN$), where the arms
differ: it excludes $\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanDef$ of the (all honest) clients in the
defect arm and $\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanFix$ in the fixed arm (difference
$\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanDiff$, fixed higher on $\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanPos$ of
$\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanN$ seeds, exact $p=\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanP$, the floor at this
$n$), and clean accuracy moves by $\pfCAFltrustDirCleanDiff$
($\pfCAFltrustDirCleanPos$ up, $\pfCAFltrustDirCleanNeg$ down). This is the
direction Corollary~\ref{cor:fltrust} predicts.
% RE-CHECK at n=10 (iteration 13: intervals invert the exact sign-flip test; at n<=5 they span [-1,1], so the text reports sign counts instead).

\textbf{What the BSR columns can and cannot say.} An attacked BSR is evidence
about a rule only against two references: the rule's own clean-model BSR (the
floor) and FedAvg's attacked BSR under the same attack (the ceiling the attack
reaches undefended). The rule fixed before the re-run (Sec.~\ref{sec:setup})
therefore reads a rule's attacked BSR only on the \emph{implant set}: the seeds on
which the re-run's own FedAvg cell implants (last-five-round BSR above $0.5$; FedAvg
runs the same computation in both arms, and a seed counts only if every FedAvg
attacked cell for it implants). So far $\pfImplantDecidedDir$ non-IID seeds have a
FedAvg attacked cell and $\pfImplantNDir$ of them implant (excluded:
$\pfImplantExclDir$); for IID, $\pfImplantNIid$ of $\pfImplantDecidedIid$
(excluded: $\pfImplantExclIid$). Over every decided seed FedAvg's attacked BSR is
$\pfBSRFedavgAllSeedsDir$ (non-IID) and $\pfBSRFedavgAllSeedsIid$ (IID), against
clean floors of $\pfBSRFedavgDirCleanFix$ and $\pfBSRFedavgIidCleanFix$. The BSR
columns of Table~\ref{tab:p1} are means over the implant set; the CA, DR and FPR
columns use every paired seed. A rule whose attacked BSR is near its floor on the
implant set suppressed the backdoor; one near FedAvg's did not.
