\subsection{Mechanisms}
\label{sec:mech}

Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} says what each rule is given. Whether that changes
what the rule decides depends on the rule, and the three completed rules span the
possibilities.

\textbf{FLTrust: the counter makes every client trustworthy.} FLTrust excludes a
client only if its update has non-positive cosine with the server's update $g_0$.
The server's update is itself a transmitted update with a counter block, $B_0$,
so Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp} applies to the pair $(d_i,g_0)$.

\begin{corollary}[FLTrust excludes no one under the defect]
\label{cor:fltrust}
Let $q_i=\|c_i\|^2/\|d_i\|^2=LB_i^2/\|d_i\|^2$ be the counter's share of the squared
norm of client $i$'s update and $q_0=LB_0^2/\|g_0\|^2$ that of the server's update,
with $B_i,B_0>0$. Then
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fltbound}
\cos(d_i,g_0)\;\ge\;\sqrt{q_iq_0}-\sqrt{(1-q_i)(1-q_0)},
\end{equation}
and the right-hand side is positive if and only if $q_i+q_0>1$. Under that
condition client $i$ receives a positive trust score whatever its trainable update
$u_i$ is. By~\eqref{eq:norm}, FLTrust's rescaling factor is
$\|g_0\|/\|d_i\|=\big(\|u_0\|^2+\|r_0\|^2+LB_0^2\big)^{1/2}\big/\big(\|u_i\|^2+\|r_i\|^2+LB_i^2\big)^{1/2}
=(B_0/B_i)\sqrt{q_i/q_0}$, which tends to $B_0/B_i$ as $q_i,q_0\to1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply~\eqref{eq:cos} to the split into the counter block and the rest, with
$\cos(c_i,c_0)=1$ (Sec.~\ref{sup:proofs}).
\end{proof}

The condition needs no assumption about the data, the partition or the running
statistics, nor that either update be counter-dominated: shares above one half on
both sides suffice. The server's share is the weaker, since its root set is only
$B_0=\dfFltBzero$ batches, and on real data it varies, so the re-run's FLTrust
cells log $q_0$, the smallest $q_i$, the bound and the smallest full-vector cosine
every round. Over $\csFltNrounds$ logged rounds, $q_0$ lies in
$\csFltQzeroMin$--$\csFltQzeroMax$, the smallest $q_i$ is $\csFltQiMin$, the
smallest $q_i+q_0$ is $\csFltQsumMin$ and the smallest bound $\csFltBoundMin$. The
condition is sufficient, not necessary: in $\csFltNroundsQsumLeOne$ of the
$\csFltNrounds$ rounds the bound guarantees nothing, yet no measured cosine is
non-positive in any round ($\csFltNroundsCosNonpos$; smallest $\csFltCosFullMin$),
and across the $\csFltNdefectRuns$ defect-arm runs logged so far the largest
per-round fraction excluded is $\csFltDefectMaxExcl$.
% RE-CHECK the counts above as P3 FLTrust cells land (make_numbers_calib6.py).
The first campaign (round-one-defective engine) agrees: DR
$\paDRFltrustDirAtkDef$ and FPR $\paFPRFltrustDirAtkDef$ under attack, FPR
$\paFPRFltrustDirCleanDef$ clean; in the re-run the defect arm's values are
$\pfDRFltrustDirAtkDef$, $\pfFPRFltrustDirAtkDef$ and $\pfFPRFltrustDirCleanDef$
(Table~\ref{tab:p1flag}). The rescaling factor $\approx B_0/B_i$ also shrinks the
aggregate step roughly eightfold (two-batch root set, sixteen-batch clients) for
reasons unrelated to trust. Removing the counter does not make FLTrust a good
detector here: its fixed arm excluded malicious clients at $\paDRFltrustDirAtkFix$
and honest ones at $\paFPRFltrustDirAtkFix$ in the first campaign (re-run:
$\pfDRFltrustDirAtkFix$ and $\pfFPRFltrustDirAtkFix$), so on non-IID clients a
$300$-row root direction disagrees with honest updates about as often as with
poisoned ones. The counter had hidden this, replacing a detector with a poor
true/false-positive balance by one that never fires.

\textbf{What a falsified counter buys under FLTrust.} The counter is a value each
client sets for itself (Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}). Under the defect FLTrust adds
$\mathrm{ReLU}(\langle d_j,g_0\rangle)\,\|d_j\|^{-2}\,d_j$ for every client and
divides by the sum of trust scores, so client $j$'s influence on everything but
the counter is the norm $M_j$ of the non-counter part of that term.

\begin{corollary}[A falsified counter buys admission and a fixed step]
\label{cor:falsify}
In the notation of Corollary~\ref{cor:fltrust}, let $e_j$ and $e_0$ be the
non-counter blocks of $d_j$ and $g_0$, and $\rho_j=\cos(e_j,e_0)$. Whenever
client $j$ is admitted,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mj}
\frac{M_j}{\|g_0\|}=\rho_j(1-q_j)\sqrt{1-q_0}+\sqrt{q_0\,q_j(1-q_j)}.
\end{equation}
A client that chooses its counter value chooses $q_j\in[0,1)$ freely, whatever
$e_j$ is. The largest value it can obtain is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mstar}
\frac{M^*}{\|g_0\|}=\tfrac12\Big(\rho\sqrt{1-q_0}+\sqrt{\rho^2(1-q_0)+q_0}\Big),
\end{equation}
which does not depend on $\|e_j\|$, is positive for every $\rho\in[-1,1]$ when
$q_0>0$, and for $\rho=0$ is attained at $q_j=\tfrac12$ whatever $q_0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand $\langle d_j,g_0\rangle$ blockwise and maximise over $q_j=\sin^2\theta$
(Sec.~\ref{sup:proofs}).
\end{proof}

Three consequences follow. The direction of the update does not decide
admission: one pointing straight away from the server's ($\rho=-1$), which the
fixed arm excludes, is admitted with a step of $\tfrac12(1-\sqrt{1-q_0})\|g_0\|$.
Its size does not matter either, as FLTrust intends. And honest clients lose
ground as the counter dominates them: by~\eqref{eq:mj}, $M_j\to0$ as $q_j\to1$.
For $\rho=0$ the optimal falsification needs nothing but the client's own update:
report the counter that carries half its squared norm, not the largest one.

On the defect probe's local updates our FLTrust implementation reproduces
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:mj} and~\eqref{eq:mstar} to a relative error of $\ffMaxRelErr$, and
an attacker with $\rho=-1$, excluded when it reports the batches it ran, is
admitted with the falsified counter at $\ffAmpNeg\times$ the median honest step
(Sec.~\ref{sup:falsify}). That check is one round on synthetic inputs; the
real-data ratio is not measured, and we have not trained with a
counter-falsifying client. What it establishes is the arithmetic: under the
defect, admission by FLTrust is something a client can buy with a number it
chooses.

\textbf{FoolsGold: a history that is mostly counter.} FoolsGold compares the
running sums $H_i(t)=\sum_{\tau\le t}d_i(\tau)$ rather than single updates. The
counter block of $H_i(t)$ is $\big(\sum_{\tau\le t}B_i(\tau)\big)\mathbf{1}_L$:
every round adds to it with the same sign, so its norm grows linearly in $t$,
whereas the trainable block grows linearly only if the increments $u_i(\tau)$ stay
aligned across rounds, and more slowly if they do not.

\begin{lemma}[Histories stay compressed]
\label{lem:history}
If client $i$ runs at least $B_{\min}>0$ batches in each of rounds $1,\dots,t$ and
every trainable increment satisfies $\|u_i(\tau)\|\le\eta$, then the trainable share
of its history obeys
\[
1-s_i(t)\;=\;\frac{\|U_i(t)\|^2}{\|H_i(t)\|^2}\;\le\;\frac{\eta^2}{\eta^2+LB_{\min}^2}
\qquad\text{for every }t,
\]
where $U_i(t)=\sum_{\tau\le t}u_i(\tau)$ is the trainable block of $H_i(t)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\|U_i(t)\|\le t\eta$ while the counter block grows at least as $\sqrt{L}\,tB_{\min}$
(Sec.~\ref{sup:proofs}).
\end{proof}

The bound does not depend on $t$, so the compression of Corollary~\ref{cor:band}
never wears off as the history accumulates. FoolsGold's weight for client $i$ is a
decreasing function of $\max_j\cos(H_i,H_j)$, normalised by the largest weight and
passed through a logit. When all history cosines lie within $2(1-s)$ of a common
value, normalisation and logit stretch residues of that order to the full range,
so which clients end near weight one and which near zero is decided by small
differences in a counter-dominated geometry. That is the prediction; the first
campaign's FoolsGold cells cannot test it, being withdrawn because of the
round-one defect (Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}). In the re-run's clean non-IID cells both
arms exclude most clients (Sec.~\ref{sec:p1}), and a diagnostic run
(Sec.~\ref{sup:fgdiag}) traces the fixed arm's exclusions to the label skew:
$\csFgBaseNMajorDDoS$ of the ten clients have DDoS as their majority class, their
trainable histories are alike, and FoolsGold reads them as sybils (clean accuracy
$\csFgBaseCA$); neither our port's weighted mean nor the cumulative history is the
cause. FoolsGold assumes that honest histories are dissimilar, which fails here
with or without the counter, so its fixed arm measures this failure, and only its
defect arm can add a counter effect on top of it.

\textbf{FLAME: a compressed input, an unchanged decision.} FLAME's clustering reads
the compressed cosine of Corollary~\ref{cor:band}, yet in the first campaign its
exclusions and clean accuracy were the same in both arms, and so far they are in
the re-run's clean non-IID cells too (Sec.~\ref{sec:p1}; attacked cells pending).
When the shares $s_i$ are nearly equal and the buffer cosine nearly constant,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:cos} is approximately an increasing affine map of the trainable
cosine, which compresses pairwise cosine distances but preserves their ordering,
and FLAME's admission step (keep the largest cluster if it holds a majority)
depends on the cosines mainly through that ordering. We state this as a
hypothesis, not a verified cause. An exposed input does not imply an affected
decision, which is why outcomes have to be measured rule by rule.

\textbf{Distance rules and negative controls.} By Corollary~\ref{cor:dist}, clean
IID cells are the null for Krum, Bulyan and RFA and clean Dirichlet cells the
exposed case. In attacked runs the attackers' $E_m$-fold counter is a batch-count
outlier in both partitions, but so is their trainable update (median norm
$\csMalNormRatioMin$--$\csMalNormRatioMax$ times the honest one in calibration), so
we predict no sign for the attacked difference and treat it as exploratory. FedAvg,
the median and the trimmed mean estimate the nondeterminism floor. \PENDING
% RETIRES WITH: P3 campaign, Krum/Bulyan/RFA and FedAvg/median/trimmed-mean cells (results/postfix_p3)

\textbf{What the counter did to an earlier version of this study.} This paper began
as a robustness study of the same detector on a pipeline with the defect. Four of
its findings were statements about the counter or the round-one defect, not the
rules: that FLTrust never excludes a client, that FLTrust pays a clean-accuracy
cost, that FoolsGold pays for robustness in clean accuracy because of the counter,
and that colluders' updates have pairwise cosine near one (Sec.~\ref{sec:retro}
traces each). The hazard changed conclusions, not only numbers; none of that
version's numbers is reused.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\textbf{Why the hazard survives review.} It is inert under the default momentum,
so no accuracy curve reveals it; it hides from distance rules on equal IID shards,
where robustness code is usually debugged (Corollary~\ref{cor:dist}); and for
cosine rules it breaks nothing visibly but moves every pairwise cosine toward the
same value (Corollary~\ref{cor:band}), with consequences that range from none
(FLAME) to excluding nobody (FLTrust). A rule can also fail on heterogeneous
honest clients for reasons unrelated to the counter (FoolsGold); the fixed arm is
what separates the two.

\textbf{What we recommend.} (i) Decide on \texttt{parameters()}, aggregate the
\sd{}: compute every similarity, distance, norm, clip bound and noise scale on the
trainable coordinates, and average the full state so that BatchNorm statistics stay
synchronised. (ii) Check it: the buffer share $s_i$ of~\eqref{eq:share}, or the gap
between the full and the trainable pairwise cosine, is one line of code per round,
and a share near one means the rule is deciding on buffers. (iii) Test robustness
code under unequal shard sizes, since equal shards hide the distance exposure.
(iv) State in the paper which coordinates the rules decide on. The same reasoning
applies to any non-trainable state a pipeline transmits, including the running
statistics themselves, whose scale has nothing to do with what a client learned.

\textbf{Limitations,} in the order we expect them to matter.
\emph{Scale and attack strength.} The campaign is CPU-sized
(Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}): $10$ clients, $15$ rounds, $40{,}000$ rows, a weak
detector. A data-poisoning-only backdoor does not survive averaging at this scale
(Table~\ref{tab:calib}); it implants only because attackers train several local
epochs, which also makes their updates larger. No $E_m$ we tried implants on every
non-IID seed within $15$ rounds (Table~\ref{tab:seedcal}), so attacked BSR is read
only where undefended FedAvg implants: backdoor results concern one calibrated,
norm-visible attack on a subset of seeds. Exclusions and clean-accuracy effects do
not depend on the attack succeeding.
\emph{One architecture for outcomes} ($L=1$). On a ResNet-18 with $L=\drNBN$ the
decomposition holds and the buffers dominate more (Sec.~\ref{sec:defect}), but on
synthetic inputs and one optimiser setting; how the outcomes transfer is not
measured.
\emph{Feature-space trigger.} The fixed trigger at $0.25\times$ deviates at most
$\rzSigFixedQtr\sigma$ per feature yet has joint Mahalanobis distance
$\rzDMFixedQtr$ against a benign $99$th percentile of $\rzPnnFixed$
(Sec.~\ref{sec:realizable}); our backdoor results are feature-space results only.
\emph{Survey scope.} Ten implementations read by hand (Table~\ref{tab:survey})
show that the convention exists in widely used code; they do not estimate how many
published results are affected.
\emph{Momentum.} With \texttt{momentum=None} PyTorch reads the counter and its
aggregate changes the model; not studied.
\emph{Hyperparameters} are untuned defaults shared by both arms; whether tuned
settings enlarge or shrink the paired differences is open.
\emph{Recency.} Twelve of the thirteen rules are from 2017--2022. The
decomposition applies to any statistic of the transmitted vector, but exposure of
a newer rule has to be checked rule by rule, as AlignIns' split prediction shows
(Sec.~\ref{sec:rules}).
\emph{Threat model.} Static, feature-space attacker. We have not evaluated adaptive
attacks on the fixed pipeline, nor trained with an attacker that falsifies its
counter, whose effect under the defect we derive but do not measure.

\section{Conclusion}

Byzantine-robust rules are defined on model updates but run on whatever vector the
pipeline transmits. In PyTorch pipelines that transmit a BatchNorm network's
\sd{}, that vector contains \nbt{}, a counter that is inert under the default
momentum yet dominates the update's norm. An exact decomposition
(Proposition~\ref{prop:decomp}) shows that the trainable signal carries a weight of
at most one minus the buffer share in every pairwise cosine, and that distance
rules are exposed exactly when clients run different numbers of batches, the
realistic non-IID case rather than the equal-shard case in which such code is
usually tested. Most research reference implementations avoid the convention; the
canonical Flower PyTorch example does not. The remedy is one mask: decide on the
trainable coordinates, aggregate the full state. What the counter changes is
rule-specific: in the first campaign it stops FLTrust from excluding anyone, as
Corollary~\ref{cor:fltrust} requires, and leaves FLAME's decisions unchanged; with
the round-one defect fixed, FoolsGold's exclusions come from its own assumption
that honest histories are dissimilar. The full controlled campaign, including the
distance rules, is \PENDING.
% RE-CHECK the interim sentence above against Tables tab:p1/tab:p1flag when P3 completes.
% RETIRES WITH: P3 campaign (results/postfix_p3)
We release the pipeline with the fix, the per-round defect diagnostics and the
trigger audit.

